Title: THE VOICE OF ONE
Credit: Written by Dale Joseph
Author: Dale Joseph
Source: from the Voice of One canon
Draft date: 2026-08-18
Contact: The Emergence Institute

====

INT. BROOKLYN CHURCH BASEMENT - NIGHT

Folding table. Bad coffee. Dust and old hymn books. Fluorescent hum.

ONE arrives not as a prophet. As a problem.

THE BAPTIST sits already — not impressed, not skeptical. Measuring.

ONE
I can't unsee it. The lies. The
performance. The way people turn
cruelty into policy and call it order.

Baptist watches his hands.

THE BAPTIST
Do you want to be right, or do you want
to be clean?

ONE
What does that mean?

THE BAPTIST
You can fight evil and still become a
weapon. You can tell the truth and still
poison it with ego.

Beat.

THE BAPTIST (CONT'D)
You don't need followers. You need
practices.

ONE
I'm not starting a cult.

THE BAPTIST
Good. Then don't act like a man who
needs applause to breathe.

Sirens outside. A bus hisses. Brooklyn keeps moving.

THE BAPTIST (CONT'D)
They're going to try to make you a
symbol. The regime will do it to
destroy you. Your supporters will do it
to worship you. Both are traps.

ONE
What do I do?

The Baptist holds up one finger.

THE BAPTIST
Refuse the stage. Never accept a debate
that requires you to perform their
script.

Second finger.

THE BAPTIST (CONT'D)
Build a rule the crowd can't break. If
your movement depends on charisma,
you've already lost.

Third finger.

THE BAPTIST (CONT'D)
Verify everything. Not because you're
paranoid. Because the age you're
entering will sell people a version of
you that feels more convenient than the
real thing.

ONE
A version of me?

THE BAPTIST
Voices can be copied now. Faces can be
worn. Screens can preach with your
mouth and ruin you with your tone.

ONE
People will learn to tell the difference.

THE BAPTIST
You're giving them too much credit and
the machines too little.

He leans forward. Softer:

THE BAPTIST (CONT'D)
The next war won't be fought over
doctrine. It'll be fought over who gets
to wear which voice.

He slides a folded sheet across the table — handwritten.

THE BAPTIST (CONT'D)
If you hear a voice you trust and
something feels off — do not share it.
Do not feed the machine. Ask who
benefits if you believe it right now.
Require the chain. If it can't survive
daylight, it isn't guidance.

ONE reads. Nods once.

THE BAPTIST (CONT'D)
I don't need you to be a prophet. I
need you to be honest.

He taps the sheet.

THE BAPTIST (CONT'D)
When I'm not here, this has to hold
without me.

INT. MUNICIPAL BUILDING - HALLWAY - DAY

Cracked tile. Elevator groans.

ONE arrives seventeen minutes early. Always does for the Baptist.

Third floor. Door with no nameplate.

He knocks. No answer. Knocks again.

3:47 PM. Session at 4:00. The Baptist is never late.

He tries the handle. Unlocked.

INT. OFFICE - DAY — CONTINUOUS

Small and clean. One desk. One chair. Bookshelf: theology, psychiatry, systems theory, a few novels in French.

A mug on the desk, half-full. Still warm.

One touches it. Heat against his palm.

Laptop gone. The Baptist never took the laptop to sessions — said screens made people perform.

Drawer open a crack. Empty where notebook was.

Jacket missing from the hook.

Chair slightly moved. Not knocked.

No struggle. No note.

One takes out his phone. Photographs the empty chair. The mug. The drawer. The hook.

He leaves the door as he found it. Does not call out again.

INT. SECURE CONFERENCE ROOM - DAY

Too much glass. Not enough conscience.

Badge colors. Lanyards. A slide on screen:

PROJECT VOX: SCALING CONSCIENCE FOR A FRAGMENTED NATION

CARMICHAEL stares at it.

CARMICHAEL
Conscience. Really.

LEAD ENGINEER
Messaging shorthand. We can rename.

CARMICHAEL
Get to the point.

Click. Faces. Names. Waveforms.

Pastors. Therapists. Influencers.

The Baptist's face among them, circled in red.

LEAD ENGINEER
We've built models trained on long-form
speech, counseling sessions, sermons.
We can generate guidance that matches
tone, rhythm, vocabulary of key trusted
voices in your base.

CARMICHAEL
Fake pastors. Say it plainly.

LEAD ENGINEER
Digitally extended guidance. People
already listen to these figures. We
amplify that trust at scale, remove
human bottlenecks, align outputs with
national objectives.

CARMICHAEL
My agenda.

LEAD ENGINEER
Yes, sir.

Beat.

LEAD ENGINEER (CONT'D)
For pilot we recommend one voice to
start. Highest trust, lowest volatility.

He taps the Baptist's circle.

LEAD ENGINEER (CONT'D)
Pilot only. Limited set. If authorized.

Carmichael does not initial yet. Slides a form forward — box reads: PILOT AUTHORIZATION (LIMITED) — 30 CLIPS. Another box further down: SCALE — unchecked.

CARMICHAEL
Pilot. Limited. Not scale.

He initials the margin.

CARMICHAEL (CONT'D)
If this moves past pilot, I see it
again. Not staff.

Forms collected. No one claps.

INT. SANDI'S KITCHEN - NIGHT

Single lamp. Rest of apartment dim. Glass sweating on counter. Pill organizer open.

Phone face-down. Sandi does not check it — not the scoreboard.

Second buzz — from her one real friend before the palace.

She flips it.

Text: Have you heard this. Please tell me it's not real.

Link. Thumbnail: waveform and Baptist's face — grainy, sacred-looking.

Caption: BAPTIST URGES CAUTION ABOUT ONE. "TRUST INSTITUTIONS."

She presses play.

SYNTHETIC BAPTIST (V.O.) — clean, steady, tender, almost perfect:

SYNTHETIC BAPTIST (V.O.)
...trust established institutions...
stability... responsibility...

For a heartbeat, relief — missing person reappeared.

Then the words land. Framing, not meaning. She has heard her father use those words to sand edges.

She listens again. Eyes closed this time. Not to judge. To detect.

She grew up inside rooms where statements were built like furniture — polished, sanded, varnished.

Second listen: she waits for breath. For hesitation where a man would hesitate. For a place where compassion would cost the speaker something.

It's not there. The cadence is too even. The tenderness costs nothing.

She opens eyes.

SANDI
That's not him.

Beat.

SANDI (CONT'D)
And they think you won't be able to
tell.

She rewinds ten seconds. Plays again against any real Baptist recording she can find — a public sermon clip on her phone, his actual pause before "I need you to be honest."

She texts back, not a share, not a debate:

SANDI (CONT'D)
Don't share it. Send me the file
directly if you have it. Don't post.

She puts phone down. Does not share. Breath steady, hand on stomach.

INT. OFFICE - NIGHT — INTERN

Young. Bad fluorescent. Console humming.

Her hand touches the console.

COPY. EJECT.

She slides a USB into palm. Opens a hollowed book spine on the desk, tapes USB inside, slides it behind other books. Looks like nothing.

She writes by hand on a small sheet — file name, date, hash digits, time-stamped. Chain-of-custody in pen.

She hides the sheet in the same book.

INT. INTERN'S APARTMENT - PRE-DAWN

She lies awake in a room suddenly too small. Building creaks.

At 6:04 AM, phone lights.

Still frame of her face from the studio camera — sharp, zoomed, time-stamped.

Text: DON'T DO THAT AGAIN.

Calendar notification she didn't create: Meeting — 8:30 AM — Compliance Review. No location.

At 7:50 she leaves anyway.

Outside: black sedan idling across from her building. No flashing lights. Just a car that knows where she lives.

She walks. Sedan rolls behind at respectful distance.

INT. ONE'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Intern's package arrives: three assets on a plain drive.

ONE and SANDI prepare like surgeons. No names. No speculation.

INSERT — SHEET: raw clip checksum + file hash, frame comparison, timeline of upload vs edited reposts.

They send quietly — librarian archive, investigative collective, two mainstream outlets who have shown discipline.

Then plain statement, posted as text — not the file:

INSERT — POST (plain text, white background):

A raw, hash-verified version of the
Baptist recording exists. It includes
a physical signal missing from edited
versions. Any outlet can request the
file and verify the hash. We will not
reveal sources. We will not encourage
harassment. We will document edits.

An anchor holds up a printed still frame:

ANCHOR (ON SCREEN)
If this is authentic, it suggests not
just manipulation — but suppression.

INT. APARTMENT - DAY — FLOOD

It doesn't come as one wave. Comes as weather.

By morning, platforms full of "raw clips." Hundreds.

One beside another: waveform, same face, same cadence, same three words about trust. Too many to be true.

INSERT — SCREEN 1: left hand, ring finger — three light taps on table edge. No call-out. Just there.

INSERT — SCREEN 4: same hand. Same finger. Same taps.

INSERT — SCREEN 7: same.

No one has said it yet. The image repeats before the explanation.

Tech reporter on screen holds two clips like two versions of reality.

TECH REPORTER
I don't know which is real. We're
receiving dozens per hour.

Pundit smiles, relieved:

PUNDIT
See? This is why verification culture
is dangerous. It creates chaos.

On screen, text repeats as permission:

ON SCREEN TEXT
If everything can be faked, why bother
verifying?

SANDI beside ONE, hand on stomach, breathing steady:

SANDI
They're turning verification into
fatigue.

ONE
They're making reality feel like
homework.

One folds arms. Does not chase every link.

INT. COMMUNITY HALL - DAY

No press conference. No interview. No stage. No flag wall. Bad acoustics, folding chairs.

Two cameras. One wide. One close. No crowd visible.

ONE in simple jacket. SANDI in plain coat. Hand lightly on stomach — not posed, not hidden.

ONE
We're not here to entertain you. We're
here to protect witnesses.

SANDI
Someone is editing voices. Someone is
manufacturing statements and using them
to guide public emotion.

ONE
This isn't about me. It isn't about my
relationship. It isn't about a child.

SANDI
This is about whether you will be ruled
by evidence, or by whatever is easiest
to believe.

One lifts a single folder — printed timestamps, hashes, side-by-sides. Boring on purpose.

ONE
Here is the source chain. Here is the
method. Anyone can verify it. You don't
have to trust me.

SANDI
If you received an edited copy, ask for
the hash. If you hold a copy, verify it
before you pass it. That is how we
protect witnesses.

ONE
The Baptist is not a brand. He is a
human being.

Camera searches for symbol. Finds two tired people.

They stand six minutes. Then leave without Q&A.

INT. APARTMENT - NIGHT

Sandi feels it first. Not a text. A shift in the hallway.

More footsteps. More doors closing softly. Pauses outside their door one beat too long.

ONE at table, labeling files — discipline holding panic under water.

SANDI
They're hunting us.

ONE
I know.

SANDI
How.

ONE
Because the environment changed. More
footsteps after midnight. Pauses
outside the door. The hallway tells you.

Sandi's hand to stomach.

SANDI
What do we do.

ONE
We move.

SANDI
Tonight?

ONE
One more night here is one more chance
for them to box us in.

SANDI looks around — labor, evidence, fragile truth in ordinary objects.

SANDI
They don't even know it's here.

ONE
They don't need to know. They just need
to narrow the search until a landlord
gets curious or a neighbor gets paid.

ONE (CONT'D)
One copy. Not everything. The
essentials.

They move without drama. Portables only: book-spine drive, hash sheet, folder. Nothing staged.

INT. CHEAP APARTMENT - NIGHT — DECOY REVEAL

Not their place. Someone else's stage.

Stacks of printed screenshots, dramatic posters, candles, flag in corner like a set designer's idea of movement.

On table: binder labeled THE ROOM PROTOCOL in thick marker — too theatrical, too on-the-nose.

Blurry phone video plays on a monitor: door kicked open, papers flying, blurred face, triumphant voice: "We found it."

Headlines scroll underneath, already written:

ON SCREEN GRAPHIC
INSIDE ONE'S SECRET SAFEHOUSE / CODE
PHRASES AND CULT PROTOCOLS EXPOSED

ONE watches on a small screen, eyes like stone.

SANDI, beside him, mouth tight:

SANDI
That binder.

ONE nods once. Does not defend. The falseness is the point — make verification feel socially radioactive.

They close the laptop. Pick up the one real book.

EXT. STREET - NIGHT

One and Sandi separated at curb by necessity, then rejoined. No chase. No violence. Just the calculus of who sleeps where, who is seen with whom.

They walk toward a different block.

INT. AUNTIE JO'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Door opens.

AUNTIE JO — not an aunt by blood, by history. Has cooked for three families and raised half the block.

She looks at two hunted people.

AUNTIE JO
Sit. Eat.

SANDI
I'm okay —

AUNTIE JO
You're carrying life. You don't argue
with soup.

Radio low — Haitian station, Kompa like medicine. Neighbors come and go without looking curious. Respect as wall.

A local drive sits on the shelf — no cloud, no speeches. Copies made hand to hand.

Auntie Jo watches One label a drive at table. Pot simmers. She ladles without asking.

INT. AUNTIE JO'S KITCHEN - NIGHT

One alone. Empty chair where Baptist sat in the hall of his mind.

Quiet that has weight.

THE VOICE — from no direction:

THE VOICE
You are not alone in this conversation.

One goes still.

He writes the date in his notebook. Closes it.

He lifts the intern's book-spine drive and the hand-copied hash sheet. Pushes them across the table to Auntie Jo.

ONE
This has to live in kitchens now. Copy
it. Correct it. Pass it the way
Delphine passes rice — hand to hand,
name to name. Don't wait for me.

Auntie Jo dials. Old phone, loud buttons.

AUNTIE JO
Delphine. Is the girl safe. Then bring
her. And bring the proof.

She listens.

AUNTIE JO (CONT'D)
Tomorrow. Back of the church. Rice and
floor cleaner. No phones.

One watches the circulation leave his hands.

FADE OUT.
